#+BEGIN_QUOTE
 Hypoxia
A condition in which oxygen supply does not match oxygen demand

 Hypoxaemia
A partial pressure of oxygen in the arterial blood below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) or haemoglobin
oxygen saturation below 90%

 Hyperoxia
elevated oxygen tension

 Acute hypoxia
Initial phase of hypoxia exposure with most pronounced (patho)physiological responses

 Chronic hypoxia
Prolonged (continuous or intermittent) hypoxia (days to years)

 Functional hypoxia
Physical exercise or extensive motor-cognitive exercise

 Continuous hypoxia
Exposure of an organism to uninterrupted hypoxia, as occurring during high-altitude

 Intermittent hypoxia
(1) high-frequency intermittent hypoxia, e.g. obstructive sleep
apnoea, elicits mostly maladaptive responses;
(2) low-frequency intermittent hypoxia, e.g. intermittent hypoxia
exposure/conditioning, elicits mostly adaptive
responses.

 Intermittent hypoxia conditioning, acute intermittent hypoxia, intermittent hypoxia training

Also termed IHE (see below), acute
intermittent hypoxia (this term is mainly used in studies on the potential of intermittent
hypoxia to modulate respiratory plasticity and/or if subjects with spinal cord injury are
exposed to intermittent hypoxia)

 Intermittent hypoxia exposure/conditioning (IHE/IHC) versus intermittent hypoxia hyperoxic exposure/conditioning (IHHE/IHHC)

repeated 4–8 min exposures to hypoxia, i.e. fractions of
inspired oxygen (FIO2 = 10–15%, alternating with 2–5 min exposures to normoxia (IHE:
FIO2 = 21%) or hyperoxia (IHHE: FIO2 = 30–35%), totalling 35–40 min/session applied 3–5
times per week for 3–8 weeks. IHHE has also been termed intermittent hypoxia
hyperoxic conditioning (IHHC)
#+END_QUOTE

** Mechanism

*** Systemic
:PROPERTIES:
:NOTER_PAGE: (8 0.3406445837063563 . 0.5235294117647058)
:END:

#+BEGIN_QUOTE
Systemic oxygen sensing
The molecular mechanisms summarized above
collaborate with and enable systemic oxygen sensing,
leading to direct hypoxia responses. The hypoxic
ventilatory response (HVR) in humans and most other
mammals increases alveolar ventilation to compensate
for reduced inspiratory PO2 and is initiated by chemo-
receptors, particularly the glomus cells of the carotid
bodies (Iturriaga et al., 2021; Teppema & Dahan,
2010). When they sense decreased arterial PO2 and
increased lactate concentration (Chang et al., 2015;
Torres-Torrelo et al., 2021), the glomus cells activate
sensory neurons of the petrosal ganglia. These neurons
transmit sensory impulses via the glossopharyngeal nerve
to specialized neurons in the nucleus tractus solitarii
(Forster & Smith, 2010) to activate the sympathetic
nervous system-mediated acute ventilatory chemo-
reflex response (Iturriaga et al., 2021). Facilitated by
pulmonary vasoconstriction, this response increases
minute ventilation and the ventilation–perfusion ratio
(Kemp et al., 2002; Pamenter & Powell, 2016).
#+END_QUOTE

**** Ventilatory response
:PROPERTIES:
:NOTER_PAGE: (10 0.5429722470904208 . 0.5229411764705882)
:END:

#+BEGIN_QUOTE
Ventilatory response and respiratory facilitation. Hypo-
xia sensing by the arterial chemosensors activates the
sympathetic nervous system and induces a ventilatory
response. IH elicits two primary forms of ventilatory
plasticity: progressive augmentation of the HVR during
IH and ventilatory long-term facilitation (vLTF) after-
ward (Puri et al., 2021). vLTF is triggered by activation of
serotonergic neurons in the raphe nuclei (Xing et al., 2014)
and modifications in the phrenic motor neurons (Nichols
& Mitchell, 2021) increasing respiratory motor output
and, thereby, minute ventilation (Pamenter & Powell,
2016).
#+END_QUOTE

**** Neuroprotection
:PROPERTIES:
:NOTER_PAGE: (10 0.20188003581020592 . 0.5229411764705882)
:END:

#+BEGIN_QUOTE
Neuroprotection. With their high oxygen demand and
limited antioxidant defences (Sorce & Krause, 2009)
neurons are highly vulnerable to ROS (Benderro et al.,
2012; Lee et al., 2005). The potential for ischaemic pre-
conditioning to improve the brain’s innate capacity to
resist and repair ischaemic injury is well recognized
(Dirnagl et al., 2009) and IH conditioning may similarly
increase the brain’s structural and functional resilience
(Burtscher, Mallet, Burtscher et al., 2021; Rybnikova
et al., 2022). Antioxidant defences bolstered by IH
conditioning may minimize neuronal vulnerability to
ROS (Burtscher, Mallet, Burtscher et al., 2021). Improved
neurogenesis is another potential IH mechanism
benefiting the brain, as demonstrated in mice (Li et al.,
2023).
#+END_QUOTE

