**** Mitochondria Benefits
:PROPERTIES:
:NOTER_PAGE: (9 0.16159355416293644 . 0.7494117647058823)
:END:

#+BEGIN_QUOTE
Prolonged continuous
hypoxia may decrease mitochondrial mass by reducing
mitochondrial biogenesis, increasing mitophagy and
altering mitochondrial morphology and dynamics
(Burtscher, Hohenauer et al., 2023), effects that
have not been well characterized for IH. Hypo-
xia/reoxygenation through aberrant fusion can produce
‘donut-shaped’ mitochondria (Liu & Hajnoczky, 2011)
or megamitochondria (Hao et al., 2023), morphologies
potentially contributing to functional recovery or auto-
phagic regulation, respectively.
The remarkable plasticity of mitochondria (oxidative
phosphorylation efficiency, substrate utilization flexibility
and reliance on alternative metabolic pathways) to
hypoxia and other stressors renders them primary
candidates to mediate beneficial hypoxia conditioning
effects. Improved mitochondrial quality control by
upregulated mitophagy and enhanced anti-oxidative
mechanisms likely contributes to improved cellular
resilience effected by hypoxia conditioning, but empirical
evidence regarding the hypoxic doses that elicit these
protective effects is scarce, particularly for IH.
#+END_QUOTE

*** Genetic
:PROPERTIES:
:NOTER_PAGE: (6 0.7936436884512086 . 0.08705882352941176)
:END:

#+BEGIN_QUOTE
Transcription factors: HIFs, Nrf2 and NF-κB. Three
powerful transcription factors mediate adaptations and/or
maladaptations to hypoxic environments. HIF-activated
genes primarily augment the blood’s oxygen-carrying
capacity via erythropoiesis, oxygen delivery to tissues
(vascular remodelling) and oxygen-independent ATP
production (glucose transport and glycolysis) (Semenza,
2012).
#+END_QUOTE

